 compounding annually at a rate of 1% for 5 years may seem tricky to compute, but we know that if it eats away at your money at just 1% a year you will also be down about \$1.00 every year.  After 5 years you will have eaten away about 5 dollars.  This means you must have about 1000 - 5 = \$950 in the account, for an interest of  \pounds 40 if you got it by multiplying 1000 by  .01, hand-calculating, etc.  We know we're wrong, because we divided 1000 by  .01, which is the reciprocal of 1%.  We might not know, but we've already calculated that by year 2, adding interest to your money still doesn't change it enough to be noticed.  That's why we rejected our wrong equals with multiplication.  What about interest?  As interest is the fact that money needs to keep growing, we add the gained interest into our account once we've gained it, and get everything into account as 1000(.01)(5) =  \pounds 50 (rounded up).  If we multiply 2 dollars a year by 5 years we get 1000 dollars.  Subtracting these two numbers,  \pounds 1050 - \pounds  1000 is $50 in interest. But we cannot just multiply 1000 by .01, here's how it works. We have that $1000 (.01) = \frac{1}{10}\cdot \frac{10}{10} \left(\frac{1}{10} \cdot1000 \right) = \frac{1}{10^2} \left(10\cdot10 \right) (100)= \frac{1000}{10^2} = \frac{1000}{100} = 10$, so how about multiplying 1000 by .01 five times?  We get  \pounds  \left(\frac{1}{10}\cdot \frac{10}{10} \left(\frac{1}{10} \cdot1000 \right) \right) \left(\frac{1}{10}\cdot \frac{10}{10} \left(\frac{1}{10} \cdot100 \right) \right) \left(\frac{1}{10}\cdot \frac{10}{10}
\left(\frac{1}{10} \cdot10 \right) \right)  \left(\frac{1}{10}\cdot \frac{10}{10} \left(\frac{1}{10} \cdot100 \right) \right) \ =\  \left(\frac{1000}{10^5} \right)= \left(\frac{10^3}{10^5} \right) = \frac{10^{-2}}  = \frac{1}{10^2}$$ We get three zeros in the denominator, we get .01 on the numerator, we get .0001 on the right hand side, rounding, the interest is $40, when actually it is $50. The correct way to solve this problem is by using the well known rule of compound interest, $A=P(1+r)^t$, where  $P$ is the principal, is the interest rate, is the number of years and $A$ is the amount of money accumulated at the end. We find, multiplying on the right hand side, $1000(.01)^5 = 1000 \left(\frac{1}{100000} \right) = \left(\frac{10^3}{10^5} \right)
=\frac{1}{10^2}=\boxed{50}$ when rounded to the nearest dollar. </think> <answer> 50 </answer>

